{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1730,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The speed of light is meant to be the ultimate\nspeed limit in the universe. According to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5580,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s special theory of relativity,\nnothing should move through space faster than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10160,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light. But that doesn’t stop people from\ntrying. Every day I get a lot of messages"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13960,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proposing ways to go faster than the speed\nof light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16860,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is the classic method where you shine\na laser at the moon. If you can flick that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20770,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beam across the moon’s surface in less than\na hundredth of a second, which is not hard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24560,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do, then that laser spot will actually\nmove across the surface of the moon faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29320,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the speed of light. Imagine what that\nwould look like if you were standing on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32930,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moon. If you were quick enough to perceive\nit, you would see this laser spot move faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37170,
    "dDurationMs": 3569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the light coming out of your own laser.\nHow is that even possible?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40739,
    "dDurationMs": 4881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, in truth, nothing here is really travelling\nfaster than the speed of light. The individual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45620,
    "dDurationMs": 3459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles of light, the photons coming out\nof my laser are still traveling to the moon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49079,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the speed of light. It is just that they\nare landing side by side in such quick succession"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53539,
    "dDurationMs": 4551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they form a spot which moves faster than\nthe speed of light. But really it is an illusion,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58090,
    "dDurationMs": 3749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing is actually going faster than the\nspeed of light. So you couldn’t transmit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61839,
    "dDurationMs": 4181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any information this way.\nDan asked: What if instead of a laser we used"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66020,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a long rigid stick instead? Now surely if\nyou flick your wrist, the tip of this stick"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71270,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must move across the surface of the moon,\nfaster than the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74670,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, unfortunately this won’t work either.\nAs we learned in the slinky drop experiment,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79980,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fastest a force can propagate through\nan object is the speed of sound, that is because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84320,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each atom needs to bump into the one next\nto it to transmit that force. And this is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88270,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lossy process. So you would be lucky if\nyou any of the energy you have put in at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92110,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start actually made it to the tip. You would\nbe lucky if the tip moved at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this is a really sophisticated idea. Gerard\nwrites: A very special space age engine would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101880,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to be designed that is capable of doing\n10,000 plus rpm in outer space with very high"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107250,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "torque. Consult Elon Musk for this. As the\nengine is spinning it slowly deploys two very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113200,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long tethers made from carbon nanotubes on\nopposing sides. Eventually each carbon nanotube"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118750,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tether reaches an amazing length of 285 kilometers.\nAt this point, the end of the tether will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124420,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be traveling at the speed of light. Can you\npoint out some reasons as to why it would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128610,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not work?\nYes, Gerard, yes, I can."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132140,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, any object going in a circle requires\na force pulling it in towards the middle of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136790,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that circle. That is called centripetal force.\nAnd you can feel it when you whirl a ball"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140790,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "above your head. Now that force is dependent\non the speed of the object squared. So if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146730,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that gets to be too great the tether breaks.\nNow if you had a single gram rotating at 99"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152700,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "percent of the speed of light, the amount\nof force required to pull it towards the center"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156250,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be 300 meganewtons. That is the weight\nof 6000 fully African elephants. But, you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162849,
    "dDurationMs": 5551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are right, carbon nanotubes are tremendously\nstrong. If you had a fiber just eight centimeters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168400,
    "dDurationMs": 2419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wide, you could support all of that force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170819,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now he problem is if you have less than\na centimeter of that fiber, it adds another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174599,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gram to the tip of you tether. And so now\nyou need a thicker fiber in order to support"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179220,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that additional force. And that would happen\nall the way to the base, so the fiber would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182980,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to get thicker and thicker and thicker\nall the way back to the motor. And if you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186620,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do the calculation you find that basically\n30 meters from the tip the fiber already has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190930,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be as wide as the observable universe in\norder to support all of that force. It is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195950,
    "dDurationMs": 2689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuts.\nBut it gets worse. As an object moves faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198639,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its inertia actually increases. That means\nit requires more force to accelerate it. In"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203790,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact, that one gram mass going 99 percent\nthe speed of light would require seven times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207660,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the amount of force we calculated before.\nAnd so the tether would have to be even thicker."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212239,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But things get even more problematic if you\nthink about speeding up the tip of the tether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216319,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that extra one percent to the speed of light.\nI mean, since the inertia keeps getting greater"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221379,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and greater, it requires more and more force\nto accelerate it. And, in fact, to speed it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226019,
    "dDurationMs": 4241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up that extra little bit to go the speed of\nlight would require an infinite amount of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230260,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy.\nOk, well putting the infinite energy aside,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233760,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s say we could create an incredible\nmotor and we could find a material much stronger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238150,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and lighter than carbon nanotube. Is it at\nleast in principle possible that the tip could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242870,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go faster than light? No. There is one final\nproblem which is insurmountable which is that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250000,
    "dDurationMs": 5609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a tether, like anything, is held together\nby the electromagnetic interaction. That is,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255609,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the attractions between all the tiny little\ncharges that makeup the material. Now the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260230,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem is, electromagnetism is a force carried\nby photons. I mean, the way that something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266060,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knows that another thing is there to attract\nit, is by the exchange of photons, these force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271250,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carrying particles. And the problem is the\nphotons themselves move at the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276620,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So even if you could create this incredible\napparatus with ridiculously strong materials"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281060,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and spin it up with infinite energy, it still\nwouldn’t go the speed of light, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286700,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the force carrying particles that hold the\nwhole thing together only go the speed of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291780,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296980,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The speed of light really is the ultimate\nspeed limit in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302080,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey, did you see that I made a video about\nthe problem with Facebook over on my second"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306260,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "channel? It really seems to have struck a\ncord, so you should check it out if you haven’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310090,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "already.\nNow I want to thank Audible for supporting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312210,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this episode of Veritasium. They are a leading\nprovider or audio books with over 150,000"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317530,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "titles in all areas of literature from fiction\nto non fiction and periodicals. Now this week"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322610,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I wanted to recommend the book by Bill Bryson\ncalled A Brief History of Nearly Everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327840,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When this book first came out I really wanted\nto dislike it, because I felt like it was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331330,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just piggy backing on Stephen Hawking’s\nBrief History of Time, but what Bill Bryson"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335640,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has done is something truly different and\nextraordinary. I really think it is a great"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340070,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "summary and a great sort of investigation\nof what happens in science. It is a brilliant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing to listen to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346390,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, if you go to Audible.com/Veritasium,\nyou can download this book for free, or another"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351540,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of your choosing. Now they actually have this\nbook in an abridged form read by Bill Bryson"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357080,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "himself. It is really interesting to hear\nthe author’s voice. To me he sounds a little"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361290,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bit like C. G. P. Gray, but with a hint of\na British accent. So you should really check"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365660,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that out. Just go to Audible.com/Veritasium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367680,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All right. Thanks for watching and thanks\nto Audible for supporting me."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371210,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are some things which are going\nfaster than the speed of light, relative to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375550,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us. There are some distant galaxies which\nare receding at a velocity than the light,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379300,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we will never be able to see the light\nthat they emit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381790,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this doesn’t violate Einstein’s theory\nof relativity, because they are not moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386060,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through space faster than light, it is just\nthat the space between us and them is expanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391100,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so quickly that their effective velocity is\ngreater than the speed of light."
    } ]
  } ]
}
